# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 bitfield

# QUIC Long Header — Initial Packet
# RFC 9000 §17.2 / §17.2.2
# Each row is 32 bits, MSB-0 numbering (bit 0 at left).

# Six of this header's fields are VARIABLE-length on the wire and v0.1 has no
# RANGE width (`BITFIELD-RANGE-WIDTH`): three connection-id/token regions and three varints.
# Until 0.1 they carried invented widths (24, 24, 24, 8, 16, 16) with
# nothing in the model to say so, and `note="padded to 24 bits for display"`
# — a renderer's excuse in a channel that does not draw. The `*` budget is one
# per block and it is spent on Payload, so the honest move available in v0.1
# is to DECLARE the fabrication: the real extent goes in the label, and the
# class meaning (which IS in the model, §12.7) says the number is a drawn
# placeholder rather than a width.
class varlen "Width is VARIABLE on the wire; the declared width is a drawn placeholder — read the real extent from the label, never from this figure's widths"

bitfield quic_initial "QUIC Long Header — Initial Packet (RFC 9000)" word=32 numbering=msb0

# ── Byte 0: First Byte (8 bits) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Abbreviated labels for 1-bit/2-bit cells (each cell is 1/32 of row width):
#   H=Header Form  F=Fixed Bit  PT=Long Packet Type  Rs=Reserved  PN=Pkt Num Len
field H:1,F:1,PT:2,Rs:2,PN:2

# NO `break` HERE, AND THIS IS LOAD-BEARING. A QUIC long header is not
# word-aligned: Version begins at bit offset 8, immediately after the first
# byte, and every field below it starts at an offset that is not a multiple of
# 32. A `break` after the first byte would end the drawing row and leave bits
# 8..31 blank. The language is right that those blank cells are not bits — but
# a reader does not read the source, and 24 blank cells inside a header row
# read as padding. That is a FALSE STATEMENT ABOUT THE WIRE, and it is a
# different thing from the `varlen` fiction below: `varlen` declares its
# placeholder in the model, whereas blank space declares nothing and is simply
# believed. Until 0.1 this file had the break, so every offset in the
# figure was 24 bits too high — a reader computed DCID Length at byte 8 when
# it is at byte 5. Do not re-add it.

# ── Version (32 bits, starting at bit 8) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Spans the end of the first 32-bit word: bits 8..39. The renderer draws a
# wrapping field as the name on the first row and `(cont.)` on the next, which
# is the RFC's own convention for a field that does not fit the remaining row.
field "Version" 32 fill=#dbeafe description="0x00000001 = QUIC v1"

# ── Destination Connection ID ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
field "DCID Length" 8
field "Destination Connection ID (0-160 bits, 0-20 octets)" 24 class=varlen description="length is carried in the preceding DCID Length field — RFC 9000 §17.2"

# ── Source Connection ID ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
field "SCID Length" 8
field "Source Connection ID (0-160 bits, 0-20 octets)" 24 class=varlen description="length is carried in the preceding SCID Length field — RFC 9000 §17.2"

# ── Token (Initial packets only) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
field "Token Length (8/16/32/64 bits, varint)" 8 present="Initial packets only" class=varlen description="RFC 9000 §16: a variable-length integer is 1, 2, 4 or 8 octets"
field "Token (variable)" 24 present="Initial packets only" class=varlen description="length is carried in the preceding Token Length field — RFC 9000 §17.2.2"

# ── Length + Packet Number ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
field "Length (8/16/32/64 bits, varint)" 16 class=varlen description="RFC 9000 §16: a variable-length integer is 1, 2, 4 or 8 octets"
field "Packet Number (8-32 bits, 1-4 octets)" 16 fill=#d1fae5 class=varlen description="length is 1 + the two-bit Packet Number Length in the first byte — RFC 9000 §17.2"

# ── Payload ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No `break` here either, for the same reason. It used to be harmless — with
# the break above, the cursor happened to land on a word edge here, so it
# added nothing. Once the first break went, this one became the same defect
# moved down the figure: it would leave the 24 bits after the Packet Number
# blank. The payload begins immediately after the packet number, so `*` fills
# the remainder of THIS row and says so.
field "Payload" * description="protected"
